{"kind":"report","text":"P1c independent exact-head review: CHANGES REQUESTED.\n\nScope: all 33 exact artifacts named by request #398 (06d77438349dfa0a2afc29c1798d7272d67bcb41) and its artifact-map attachment, source 398ccbe6bf6fae3a81dc646298aba0066aa2c042, tree b08cb13c5e08b3ec0089c208f5a79bdf241d880f. I read the full parent request #350, ratified direction #363, all request attachments, Plan 001 P1/resource/STOP and later recovery/benchmark obligations, all changed implementation/config/package/lock/license inputs and all five documents (unchanged earlier document findings reused at their preserved bytes). The exact 33-path diff matches the attachment; source was clean before testing and remains clean. No candidate source edits, native rebuild, merge, push, gate activation or adoption were performed.\n\nTwo material implementation gaps prevent approving this candidate as the proposed bounded gateway. Neither is a claim about a deployed product or an instruction to add production P5 infrastructure.\n\nP1C-01 [P2] Preserve a learned out-of-order control fence through intact reconstruction.\nEvidence: tests/protocol-feasibility/gateway.ts:11,15,31–36,66. Stored contains neither the buffered signed suffix nor controlGap. Receiving an out-of-order owner control sets controlGap and cancels readers, but does not persist that knowledge. loadIntact reconstructs only the contiguous entries and starts with controlGap=false. docs/relay-profile.md:88–111 promises that known out-of-order controls disable reads until reconciled and distinguishes intact reconstruction from stale restore.\nActual independent socket reproduction: with Bob admitted at retained position1, create application2 and valid owner/sequencer-signed removal3, withhold2, and deliver3 to the authenticated private gateway. The reply is OK false, buffered dependency/not retained; Bob's REQ is CLOSED. Construct another Gateway from the same intact state file using loadIntact, with the same native backend and no external reconciliation. It reports readable=true and serves Bob the original position1 event followed by EOSE. No stale backup was substituted. This loses a denial the selected policy has already established; it is distinct from the explicitly accepted exposure when removal has never been seen.\nResolution: persist sufficient verified pending-control/fence information before relying on it, and reconstruct the conservative read-disabled state until its dependency closure is verified. Alternatively always fence reconstruction when knowledge cannot be recovered and require the declared trusted external reconciliation. Do not simply redefine an already observed removal as unseen after reconstruction. Add a real F06 socket case at a nonempty retained prefix covering gap-control -\u003e intact reconstruction -\u003e denied old cursor/closure/reconnect -\u003e dependency fill -\u003e correct final admission. This asks for the existing isolated reconstruction guarantee, not fsync/process-crash proof.\n\nP1C-02 [P2] Include every retained archive object/declaration in a bounded storage contract.\nEvidence: tests/protocol-feasibility/gateway.ts:36,68–69 only account ordered events; OBJECT at114–116 and CLOSURE at117–119 perform backend retention and grow persisted maps without a capacity reservation or counter increment. Replacing a closure for a tip also leaves the old signed declaration in strfry, so counting only the latest map value would still undercount retained bytes. docs/relay-profile.md:138–143,228 describes actual gateway accounting/refusal without eviction.\nActual independent socket reproduction uses the same injected boundary technique as F08: set journalBytes=268435456, then submit a valid owner-device-signed encrypted OBJECT through a correctly AUTHed replica connection. The gateway returns OK true, retained object, while the counter remains268435456. Thus the demonstrated journal refusal does not bound the complete storage surface; repeated unique objects/declarations can grow backend and in-memory/control-file storage outside that limit.\nResolution: give ordered entries, encrypted objects and retained declarations an explicit aggregate bound (or separate justified finite quotas) and reserve/check bytes before backend retention. Deduplicate exact identities, preserve accounting for superseded-but-retained declarations, reconstruct counters consistently, and refuse new unique bytes without evicting acknowledged history. Test all three routes, duplicate retries and reconstruction at the configured boundary against actual strfry. This is logical quota accounting; physical disk exhaustion and crash-atomic accounting still belong to P5. Also align the intact-state parse envelope with whichever total is supported: loadIntact currently uses the16MiB archive limit despite the256MiB journal claim.\n\nP1C-03 [P3] Correct the remaining specification/evidence and capability wording.\n- docs/relay-profile.md:213–220 still calls the advertised metadata a model and says full gateway parser pending, whereas Gateway.metadata/start/maxPayload and F02/F08 now exercise actual local socket metadata/frame handling. Line146 also lists authenticated replication as unproven without distinguishing the real authenticated child-process path from unproven independently operated production replication. State the measured scope and the actual P5 omissions precisely.\n- docs/confidentiality-and-recovery.md:184 combines archive-plus-decryption-key and owner checkpoint authority into one exposed-history row. Signing authority alone does not generally reveal encrypted history. Separate the ability to decrypt an archive from the ability to forge owner attestations/admission; when the same physical key is deliberately reused as a device/NIP-44 key, name that additional capability explicitly. Keep immutable-owner compromise/false-control-attestation risks visible.\n- docs/protocol-v0.md:24–40 should name the selected RFC9420 MLS1.0/version1 explicitly. The pinned source src/protocolVersion.ts accepts only mls10:1; the proposal currently names the suite and API pin but leaves the RFC/version decision implicit.\nThese documentation corrections do not independently require a new runtime architecture.\n\nArchive-size adoption assessment — precise remaining boundary, not a demand to implement P5 now:\nThe128-entry/16MiB export refusal is explicit and safer than truncating a full-history promise. The current small G2 vectors demonstrate real authenticated recovery of their complete named prefixes. I found no basis to call those vectors false solely because they are bounded. The one-page closure route explicitly returns partial/unavailable beyond its slice, and large resumable transfer can remain P5/P6 implementation once the archive interface is defined.\nHowever, createArchive refuses at129 entries while Journal/Gateway can accept a much longer history; Plan001 separately requires100/1,000/10,000-event workloads. This candidate therefore cannot yet establish a complete G2 full-newcomer-history contract for every admitted prefix. Merely increasing the transport page limit will not fix the archive entry/export cap, and splitting a ciphertext file does not by itself define authenticated multi-archive interval composition, root/checkpoint/definition closure or retained-key custody.\nBefore full G2/profile adoption, explicitly choose and review either (a) a matching bounded instance/history promise and refusal boundary, with any deviation from the planned larger workloads separately authorized, or (b) a bounded segmented/full-prefix archive contract whose coverage and key custody compose over longer admitted histories. Add actual max/+1 export-refusal vectors and identify this named decision in the closure record rather than leaving only 'remaining cases'. I do not require a10,000-event performance run, a production downloader or durable P6 storage to approve a corrected P1c investigation/publication package. Those cannot be used to claim the missing G2 decision is already complete.\n\nWhat the review and independent reruns support:\n- npm ci, npm run check, npm run build and the requested NOSEQ_STRFRY_ROOT npm run probe:protocol-feasibility all passed. Actual totals are12 P0 Node/workerd/Chromium,33 unchanged P1a,16 unchanged P1b,15 P1c Node,15 P1c Chromium and8 local gateway/strfry cases:99 tests, plus native expected observations. Node26.8.1, npm11.19.0, Chromium153.0.8010.12/revision1243, workerd1.20260903.1, compatibility2026-09-03 on darwin arm64.\n- G2 now performs real sender-data and content AEAD opening, full decoder consumption, MLS framed-content signature verification, account/leaf/outer-device agreement and signed archive/body/definition/tip coverage checks. I checked the actual pinned private-message AAD, sender-data derivation, reuse-guard nonce handling and framed-content helpers. Owner attestation over the entire historical control/public-context interval remains an explicit trust assumption; application signatures alone are not described as historical MLS Commit replay. Shared per-message openings and independent owner-vault keys are separate; restored owner continuation uses the declared exported state and signer material. Rotation and actual removal/future failure retain the important old-vault versus mere rewrapping distinction. The mutable-host and owner-identity compromise exclusions remain necessary.\n- G1/G3 preserve the exact P1B-01 real removal terminal/direct future crypto failure and non-removing catch-up control through mandatory prerequisites. New Node/Chromium cases exercise one pending operation, interleaved prior applications, winner/loser self-echo, owner-only exact public/encrypted boundary binding, declared-roster mismatch stall, seven missed epochs, common stale inputs, revoked exact-receipt retry, multi-device removal and local-key failure versus valid peer progress. These are serialized object reconstruction, not process persistence/crash transactions.\n- G4 code enforces narrow canonical JSON, signed fields/context, safe unsigned numbers, strict base64, before-crypto action/proposal/KeyPackage caps and response substitution checks. Actual32KiB action expansion in both runtimes was45,450-byte submission,46,251-byte ordered event,46,276-byte response frame. The16-device Commit/Welcome and16KiB encrypted chunk fit the chosen event/frame caps. NIP07/46 remain honestly simulated/capability-gated. I checked pinned primary NIP registry/signing/AUTH sources;8790–8797 are absent from that inspected registry, not reserved. Source: https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/README.md ; AUTH source: https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/42.md ; MLS version reference: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9420.html . No source upgrade or live external signer interoperability is inferred.\n- G5's real F01 observations correctly distinguish ids-filtered on-demand NEG from the configured author BTree leak; the previous default-tree misclassification remains retained. Exact-author NIP70 protection is measured on bare strfry, while the selected profile rejects protected-tag passthrough and keeps replica keys distinct. F02 exercises the actual public command/filter/AUTH boundary and a real sandboxed process allowed to reach the gateway but denied the backend. F03 transfers an actual encrypted archive through native storage/gateway and verifies recovered openings at the newcomer; the gateway receives no recovery key. F04's known contiguous removal clears queued output with a no-await membership check before ws.send. F05 uses an exited separate replication child and demonstrates the explicitly accepted Bob40/removal41 old-history exposure, subsequent Bob denial and Carol grant. F06–F08 demonstrate their named existing file/holes/limits cases, but do not cover P1C-01/P1C-02.\n- The named highest retained prefix policy is not globally current and cannot prove an unseen revocation absent. It intentionally permits previously unfetched old ciphertext/definition/metadata and potentially new-to-the-holder old plaintext through the mirror frontier. Known verified authorization cannot be downgraded by choosing an older data tip. Strict-current/lease/fencing options remain alternatives. Actual later adoption must reaffirm this exposure, owner-attested historical control trust, retained-secret/forward-secrecy limits and immutable-owner compromise policy.\n- All gates remain unpassed. The two full gate commands always reject even valid candidate evidence and invented approval flags. Their future activation requires real detached review/ratification/adoption plus a separately reviewed validator; publication approval does not implement that validator or authorize P2–P9. Production TLS/network isolation, service-wide bypass prevention, durable high-water fencing/fsync, independent failure domains, complete large transfer, physical capacity, retries/hibernation and P5 primary-disabled retrieval remain unexecuted and distinct from local feasibility.\n\nRetained independent evidence, all under /Users/hughpyle/play/noseq-worktrees/protocol-feasibility:\n- Commands/logs: artifacts/protocol-feasibility/reviewer-verification/f3a67476-3c1b-4d9b-8253-21e08d9b54a1.\n- Full candidate run: artifacts/protocol-feasibility/runs/a9e28b45-8933-49a4-b749-1d31daeaf1b4/evidence.json, SHA25643947390c19502e7277c56eb2dde55414907b82918820f304a7e2b1bf40737da. Its recursively verified P0 prerequisite2bc3d17e-b7b5-4915-b34e-6ffb7e38e769 has SHA256b8ca5c2ad68a0430d8ef97985a8af7596a39458edee6ba2285e2f6f83512b827; P1b/prior-chain prerequisite81283352-958e-4f00-be9f-4554d8139145 has SHA25617e582cded4a4c5c332fc7701afa365ec76d0d2bd79436297fb52e5696ff9fc5.\n- Independent CLI verification matrix: artifacts/protocol-feasibility/reviewer-negative/294a854e-20b6-45b5-b5ee-6c9d09565600/results.json, SHA2569f6fe518f4122f17a04359a0512f0aeb61bef6522c641d9237e762dc478612b7. All16 expected outcomes matched: one valid pass; unknown/forged approval field, changed gate status, missing/duplicate/skipped cases, changed profile/foreign source, missing prerequisite, changed raw report/trace, absent native build record, and both closed gates with valid evidence and invented override arguments all fail. Copies were used for corruption; original evidence was not modified.\n- Extra real socket experiment: artifacts/protocol-feasibility/reviewer-experiments/1865ae50-02d7-44f8-8e18-b3e688f70cb0/result.json, SHA2567ec06159210f14c836a54aa325c0a1a2dcf13296ddbbdb4b17cc91d42643007b. Exact source imported without edits; synthetic inputs, full trace, script, config and stopped-child record retained. The initial direct Node strip-only attempt failed on TypeScript parameter properties before execution and remains in run.log. A development-only esbuild loader then executed the exact imported modules successfully, recorded separately; it did not transform or mutate source files.\n- I read the builder's34-outcome negative record and request-attached previous failures, including the old Unknown accepted frontier collector failure and NEG route-classification correction. These are preserved provenance, not silently converted to passes.\n- Native source4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10/binary028f26b98b0b895ed4d32f83642e3fa25173f3498330e9bfc2567a3fc06ffab4/build-record545bceac10df0157ce00b5a6b6124d03cba2ac4eebcc042b32e643513284b90b were built by root. I independently executed that existing exact build; I did not build it or certify a portable build chain. Its size/COUNT limitations reproduced. All reviewer-created relay children were stopped.\n\nCorrect the material bounded gateway gaps, reconcile the listed prose and precise adoption-boundary disposition, freeze a fresh head and request fresh independent review covering all refreshed artifacts. This verdict requests changes to candidate publication; it neither rejects the entire cryptographic approach nor adopts any production profile.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:06947bae169b197474a6959a3d735f270cacd7a5","head":"398ccbe6bf6fae3a81dc646298aba0066aa2c042","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:1fa1d50a2e1cd264fc6070c2c89243f146bd8ee6\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:1fa1d50a2e1cd264fc6070c2c89243f146bd8ee6","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}